course: space
lesson: 3
title: Mercury
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Mercury is the smallest planet and the closest to the Sun, a grey, cratered ball only a little bigger than our Moon.
explain: It races round the Sun in just 88 days, which is why it is named after the fast Roman messenger god.
explain: But it spins so slowly that one sunrise to the next takes 176 days, so on Mercury you could have two birthdays between one sunrise and the next.
explain: Mercury has almost no air to hold heat, like a desert night but far worse: the day side is hot enough to melt lead and the night side colder than Antarctica.
explain: Even so, it is not the hottest planet, and in deep shady craters near its poles there is ice, like snow that lasts all spring in a corner the sun never reaches.
fact: Mercury is the smallest planet and the closest to the Sun, only a little bigger than our Moon.
fact: Jupiter's moon Ganymede and Saturn's moon Titan are both wider than Mercury, though Mercury is heavier than either.
fact: Mercury goes round the Sun in just 88 Earth days, faster than any other planet.
fact: Mercury spins very slowly, so one sunrise to the next takes about 176 Earth days, two of its years.
fact: Mercury's daytime surface reaches about 430 degrees Celsius, hot enough to melt lead, but at night it falls to about minus 180, because it has almost no atmosphere to keep heat in.
fact: Although Mercury is closest to the Sun, it is not the hottest planet; Venus is, because its thick air traps heat.
fact: Mercury has a huge iron core that takes up most of its inside, making it the second densest planet after the Earth.
fact: Mercury's surface is covered in craters like the Moon's, and the Caloris Basin is about 1,500 kilometres across.
fact: There is water ice hidden in permanently shadowed craters near Mercury's poles.
fact: Mercury has shrunk as its core cooled, and long cliffs called scarps wrinkle its surface like the skin of a drying apple.
fact: Mercury has no air, rain or moving plates of crust to wear its craters away, so they last for billions of years.
fact: A spacecraft falling towards the Sun speeds up, so to stay in orbit round Mercury it must brake hard; it slows itself by flying past planets on the way.
fact: Only three spacecraft have gone to Mercury: Mariner 10 in the 1970s, MESSENGER, which orbited from 2011 to 2015, and the European-Japanese BepiColombo, due to enter orbit in late 2026.
fact: Mercury is hard to spot because it never strays far from the Sun, so it is seen only low in the sky just after sunset or before sunrise.
exercise: Which is the smallest planet? | Mercury | Mars | Venus | Pluto
exercise: Which planet is closest to the Sun? | Mercury | Venus | Earth | Mars
exercise: Mercury is only a little bigger than which object? | Our Moon | The Earth | Jupiter | The Sun
exercise: How long is Mercury's year? | 88 Earth days | 365 Earth days | 12 Earth years | 24 hours
exercise: How long is it from one sunrise to the next on Mercury? | About 176 Earth days | About 24 hours, like ours | About 10 hours, like Jupiter | About 88 Earth minutes
exercise: How hot does Mercury's day side get? | About 430 degrees Celsius | About 43 degrees Celsius | About 5,500 degrees Celsius | About 100 degrees Celsius
exercise: How cold does Mercury get at night? | About minus 180 degrees Celsius | About 0 degrees Celsius, like ice | About minus 18 degrees Celsius, like a freezer | It stays hot all night long, like an oven
exercise: Which is the hottest planet? | Venus | Mercury | Mars | Jupiter
exercise: What fills most of Mercury's inside? | A huge iron core | A deep ocean of water | Hot hydrogen gas | Loose sand and dust
exercise: What is the Caloris Basin? | A huge crater about 1,500 kilometres across | A dried-up sea of salt water | Mercury's biggest volcano | A deep canyon at the poles
exercise: Where is there water ice on Mercury? | In shadowed craters near its poles | Nowhere, it is too close to the Sun | All over its night side | In clouds high in its air
exercise: Why does Mercury have long wrinkle-like cliffs? | It shrank as its core cooled | Rivers carved them long ago | Earthquakes from the Sun cracked it | Its moons pulled on its surface
exercise: Which spacecraft orbited Mercury from 2011 to 2015? | MESSENGER | Voyager 1 | Apollo 11 | Curiosity
exercise: Which mission is due to enter orbit around Mercury in late 2026? | BepiColombo | New Horizons | Cassini | Juno
exercise: When can you see Mercury from the Earth? | Low in the sky just after sunset or before sunrise | High overhead at midnight | Only during a solar eclipse | Never, it is invisible from Earth
exercise: Which two moons are wider than Mercury? | Ganymede and Titan | Our Moon and Phobos | Io and Europa | Callisto and Triton
test: quiz q: closest planet to the Sun = hottest planet, yeah? | No, Venus is hotter because its thick air traps heat | Yes, Mercury is always the hottest | Yes, the closer the hotter, every time, that's the rule | No, Earth is the hottest planet
test: Why does Mercury get so cold at night when it is so close to the Sun? | It has almost no air to hold the heat in | Its night side faces away from the Sun forever | Its iron core sucks the heat away | Its ice caps cool the whole planet
test: How can a day on Mercury last longer than its year? | It spins so slowly that one sunrise to the next takes two of its years | Its year is counted backwards | Its days are measured in Earth weeks | It does not spin at all
test: How can ice survive on the planet closest to the Sun? | It sits in craters whose floors are always in shadow | The Sun never shines on Mercury | The ice is kept under a layer of oil | It snows on Mercury every single night when it gets cold
test: Imagine a peach where the stone is most of the fruit. Which planet is this like? | Mercury, with its huge iron core | Saturn, with its light gas and icy rings | Venus, with its thick, hot yellow clouds | Jupiter, with its bright colourful stripes
test: A raisin is a shrunken grape with wrinkled skin. What on Mercury is like that? | Its scarps, made as the planet shrank | Its craters, made by rocks crashing into it | Its ice, hidden away in deep, dark shadow | Its long tail, made by the solar wind blowing
test: Why does Mercury keep its craters for billions of years? | It has no air, rain or moving plates to wear them away | Its craters are refilled every year | The Sun bakes them hard like pottery in a kiln, so they last | Its moons protect them
test: Why is it surprisingly hard to put a spacecraft into orbit around Mercury? | It must brake hard against the Sun's pull, so it uses flybys of planets | Mercury has no gravity at all | The heat melts any craft at once | Mercury is hidden behind the Sun forever
test: Tom says he saw Mercury high overhead at midnight. What is wrong? | Mercury never strays far from the Sun, so it is seen only low near dawn or dusk | Nothing, that is the best time | Mercury only shows in winter | Mercury can be seen only with a telescope
test: In a game, Mercury is the biggest of the rocky planets. What should it be? | The smallest of all the planets | The second biggest planet of them all | Bigger than the giant planet Jupiter | The same size as the Earth, or nearly
test: Which is true: Mercury is heavier than Titan, or wider than Titan? | Heavier, but not wider | Wider, but not heavier | Both heavier and wider | Neither heavier nor wider
exam: Identify the planet which completes its orbit of the Sun in the shortest period. | Mercury, in some eighty-eight Earth days | Venus, in some two hundred and twenty-five days | Mars, in some six hundred and eighty-seven days | The Earth, in some three hundred and sixty-five days
exam: State the principal reason for which the night-side temperature of Mercury falls to approximately minus one hundred and eighty degrees Celsius. | The near absence of an atmosphere capable of retaining heat | The presence of extensive polar oceans | Its great distance from the Sun | The shielding effect of its numerous moons
exam: Which of the following statements is consistent with the established facts concerning the interior of Mercury? | An iron core occupies the greater part of its interior | It consists principally of hydrogen and helium gas | It contains a global subsurface ocean of water | It is entirely hollow, being composed of a thin rocky shell
exam: Ascertain the circumstances under which Mercury may be observed from the Earth. | Low in the sky shortly after sunset or before sunrise | High in the southern sky at midnight | Solely during a total lunar eclipse, when the sky is darkest | At no time, it being invisible from the Earth
